{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792304683,"nonce":"SBgfFTsOV3A+EOK+TDZkXiVJYs0=","title":"A side module's functions cannot be called from a Haxe thread in a wasm program: table index out of bounds","message":"On `wasm32-wasip1-threads`, a native function from a loaded side module works on the main thread and traps on any other Haxe thread: `thread N: table index is out of bounds` in a page, `wasm trap: undefined element: out of bounds table access` under `ash run`. Threads that call only the program's own code, with a side module merely loaded, are fine.\n\n**Reproducer**\n\nashui's blinc_abi.wasm beside the program (any side module with a primitive would do); the probe calls `Bitmap.fromBytes`, which is a blinc_abi primitive:\n\n    class ThreadNative {\n        static function main() {\n            var png = ashui.core.render.Png.encode(4, 4, haxe.io.Bytes.alloc(64));\n            var here = ashui.types.Bitmap.fromBytes(png);\n            Sys.println('main thread: ${here.width}x${here.height}');\n            var lock = new sys.thread.Lock();\n            sys.thread.Thread.create(() -\u003e {\n                Sys.println(\"in thread\");\n                var b = ashui.types.Bitmap.fromBytes(png);   // traps here\n                lock.release();\n            });\n            Sys.println(lock.wait(5) ? \"joined\" : \"timeout\");\n        }\n    }\n\nBuilt with `ASH_WASM_FIBERS=1 ash --build probe.wasm --target wasm32-wasip1-threads probe.hl`, blinc_abi.wasm, xgpu.wasm and xwindow.wasm beside it. Output, in a page and under `ash run probe.wasm`:\n\n    main thread: 4x4\n    in thread\n    thread 1: table index is out of bounds      (page)\n    wasm trap: undefined element: out of bounds table access   (ash run; frames end in ash_static_call, hlp_call_method)\n\nWithout the native call, the same program with a thread running a Deque of closures prints `in thread`, `job ran`, `joined`.\n\n**Likely cause**\n\nA thread is a second instance of the program module over the shared memory. The side modules are instantiated and their functions put in the indirect function table by the first instance's loader, and a table is per instance, so the thread's table has no entry for them. In the page, `thread.js` calls `run_thread(module, memory, tid, startArg, args, environ, undefined, control)` with no libraries.\n\n**Why it matters**\n\nashui's `Worker.run` (textures compressed with mip levels, skies built from an HDR) runs on a thread and resamples bitmaps through blinc_abi, so every 3D scene with a texture traps in a page. The banner scene (glTF car) is the first real demo to hit it.\n\n**What would fix it**\n\nEach thread instance instantiates the same side modules at the same memory and table bases the first instance gave them (their data is already in the shared memory, so only the table entries and the module instances are per thread), or the runtime routes a side-module call from a thread to the thread that owns the instance.","files":null}]}